{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>If she is evil in the eyes of her master, who did not designate her.</b> To take her as a wife. <b>Ramban wrote</b>, of blessed memory, that one who buys an Israelite daughter to take her as a wife buys her and she is designated to him by presumption. And now, if he does not desire her and the father redeemed her as mentioned, for it is forbidden for him to leave her any longer in the master's hand from the time he said 'I did not desire to take her'; and he cannot sell her to a foreign people — and this is a total prohibition against a foreigner, that one should not sell his daughter to a Cuthean as a maidservant. For even though Scripture permitted this regarding a Hebrew slave, as it says (Leviticus 25:47) ״ונמכר לגר תושב עמך״, Scripture comes to explain that one must not do so with a woman, because the Cutheans should not come to degrade the daughters of Israel. And this is the plain sense of the verse, that a person should not sell his daughter to a Cuthean as a maidservant — end of quotation." "<b>If he has dealt deceitfully with her.</b> He has betrayed his daughter if he sells her to anyone other than one who could marry her as a wife. And in tractate Kiddushin, chapter one, Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Akiva disagreed concerning this word בבגדו: one understood it from the language of betrayal (בגידה) and one from the language of garment (בגד). Rabbi Akiva held that there is significance to the received reading (יש אם למקרא), for since he spread his cloak over her he may no longer sell her; and Rabbi Eliezer held that there is significance to the consonantal tradition (יש אם למסורת). Rabbi Akiva understood the word בבגדו as from the language of garment (בגד) literally, as in (Genesis 39:12) ״ותתפשהו בבגדו״; and therefore he said: since he spread his cloak over her — and that is kiddushin, in the manner of what is written (Ruth 3:9) ״ופרשת כנפיך על אמתך״. For had it been from the language of betrayal (בגידה), Scripture ought to have said בבגדו with a hatef-qamatz under the bet; but it is the language of garment (בגד) and cloak. And what it says 'in her' (בה) is like 'over her,' in the manner of what is written (Nehemiah 2:12) ״הבהמה אשר אני רוכב בה״, which is like 'over her.' And Rabbi Eliezer understood the word בבגדו from the language of betrayal (בגידה), and even though it did not say בבגדו with a hatef-qamatz under the bet, but said בבגדו with a ḥiriq under the bet — it is nevertheless from the language of betrayal (בגידה), and it is in the construct form, like (Leviticus 26:26) ״בשברי לכם מטה לחם״; and therefore it used the expression 'in her' (בה), for the language of betrayal (בגידה) we find only with a bet — as it is written (Malachi 2:15) ״אשר אתה בגדתה בה״, and it is written (ibid.) ״ובאשת נעוריך אל יבגוד״, and it is written (Hosea 5:7) ״בה׳ בגדו כי בנים זרים ילדו״. And Rabbi Shimon held that there is significance both to the received reading and to the consonantal tradition, and we interpret them all both in the language of garment (בגד) and in the language of betrayal (בגידה)." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> ״If a man sells his daughter as a maidservant״ — the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Israel: I had one daughter and I sold her to you as a maidservant, for you do not bring her out except confined in a chest. ״She shall not go out as slaves go out״ — treat her with honor, for you captured her from Me, as it is said (Psalms 68:19) ״עלית למרום שבית שבי״. ״If she is evil in the eyes of her master״ — if Israel are evil and do not fulfill the Torah. ״And the father redeemed״ — that is, the Shekhinah withdraws from them. And since at the giving of the Torah to a foreign people he has no authority — ״to sell her״: it was not given to any nation of the world but only to Israel, and Israel betray her."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:02:20.467292637Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}